---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Compressor from '../components/Compressor.astro';
import SEOContent from '../components/SEOContent.astro';

const SEO_TITLE = "Image Compressor to 50KB — Compress Image to 50 KB Online";
const SEO_DESC = "Use our free Image Compressor to 50KB. Compress image to 50 KB online ensuring the highest quality while staying exactly under the size limit.";
---

<Layout title={SEO_TITLE} description={SEO_DESC}>
  <section class="relative z-20 pt-20 container-stripe">
    
    <!-- Header -->
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 48px;">
      <div
        style="
          display: inline-flex;
          align-items: center;
          gap: 8px;
          background: rgba(200,241,53,0.08);
          border: 1px solid rgba(200,241,53,0.2);
          border-radius: 100px;
          padding: 5px 14px;
          margin-bottom: 24px;
        "
      >
        <span
          style="
            font-family: var(--font-mono);
            font-size: 0.6875rem;
            font-weight: 700;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 0.1em;
            color: var(--color-lime);
          "
        >Target Size: 50KB</span>
      </div>

      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 8vw, 4.5rem); line-height: 1; margin: 0 0 24px; letter-spacing: -0.05em;">
        Image Compressor to <br/>
        <span style="color: var(--color-lime);">Exactly 50KB.</span>
      </h1>
      <p style="font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--color-ink-2); line-height: 1.6; max-width: 540px; margin: 0 auto;">
        Our smart engine resizes and optimizes your photo to stay perfectly under the 50KB threshold with maximum possible clarity.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The Tool -->
    <Compressor defaultKB={50} hideHeader={true} />

    <SEOContent 
      targetKB="50" 
      customTitle="Compress Image to 50KB: The Ultimate Guide for Indian Government Exams and Official Portals"
      customFaqs={[
        {
          q: "Why do Indian government portals specify exactly 50KB for photo uploads?",
          a: "Portals like UPSC, SSC, and various state government recruitment boards handle millions of applications. Capping the photo size at 50KB reduces server load and storage costs while ensuring the image retains just enough resolution for facial recognition and admit card printing."
        },
        {
          q: "How can I make my signature clearly visible under 50KB?",
          a: "For signatures, crop out all excess white space and increase the contrast before compressing. Since signatures use very few colors, our 50KB compressor can retain sharp, crisp ink lines without pixelation, provided the background is purely white."
        },
        {
          q: "What are the optimal starting dimensions for a 50KB headshot?",
          a: "To achieve the best clarity at 50KB, start with dimensions between 140x180 pixels and 200x250 pixels. Starting with excessively large dimensions (like 4000x3000) forces the compressor to drop too much data, resulting in a blurry final image."
        },
        {
          q: "Will resizing to 50KB change the physical dimensions (width/height) of my photo?",
          a: "Our primary 50KB compression process only reduces the file size (data footprint) by optimizing metadata and JPEG compression. However, if your image's original dimensions are exceptionally large, we dynamically apply smart scaling down to an optimal pixel size to guarantee maximum visual clarity under 50KB."
        },
        {
          q: "Is a 50KB image considered high quality?",
          a: "For digital application forms and ID cards, 50KB is perfectly adequate for a passport-sized headshot. However, for printing large posters, landscapes, or high-end photography, 50KB is too compressed. Quality is relative to the use case."
        }
      ]}
      uniqueContent={`
        <p class="seo-p">
          If you are a student or a professional in India, you are undoubtedly familiar with the strict file size limits on official websites. The <strong>50KB limit</strong> is highly specific and undeniably the "Golden Standard" for Passport-sized Photographs and Signature uploads across numerous Indian government portals.
        </p>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">Why 50KB Specifically? The Standard for Indian Government Portals</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          The 50KB requirement isn't arbitrary. National and state-level recruitment boards—such as the <strong>Union Public Service Commission (UPSC)</strong>, the <strong>Staff Selection Commission (SSC)</strong>, the <strong>Institute of Banking Personnel Selection (IBPS)</strong>, and <strong>NEET</strong> exam portals—process millions of applications annually. Storing massive, uncompressed images from millions of candidates would lead to astronomical server costs and slow loading times for administrators. 
        </p>
        <p class="seo-p">
          A file size capped at precisely 50KB provides the perfect balance for these organizations. It ensures the uploaded passport photo retains enough facial detail to verify a candidate's identity on an admit card, while remaining small enough to guarantee rapid database queries. Furthermore, it is the strict upper boundary for PAN card corrections, Voter ID updates, and many Aadhaar-related online KYC submissions.
        </p>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">Real-World Examples of 50KB Constraints</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          To illustrate how rigid these systems are, let's look at real-world scenarios. A typical <strong>UPSC application asks for a photo under 50KB with a minimum resolution of 140x180 pixels</strong> (and sometimes capped at 350x350 pixels). If you upload a file that is 51KB, the automated portal will instantly reject it with an "Invalid File Size" error. Similarly, SSC portals generally demand your passport photo to strictly sit between the 20KB to 50KB range. This lack of leeway is precisely why our 50KB image compressor was designed to hit the target with mathematical precision, preventing upload rejections.
        </p>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">The Challenge of 50KB: Balancing Quality With Aggressive Compression</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          Compressing a modern smartphone photo—which often averages around 3MB to 5MB (or 3000KB to 5000KB)—down to a mere 50KB requires reducing its data footprint by over 98%. This is categorized as aggressive compression. 
        </p>
        <p class="seo-p">
          The primary challenge here is avoiding the "mosaic effect" or heavy pixelation. Most generic online tools simply crush the image data blindly, resulting in a blurry face, illegible signature, or color banding on the background. Our 50KB optimizer overcomes this by utilizing a multi-pass algorithmic approach. We selectively strip unnecessary EXIF metadata, optimize color quantization, and dynamically adjust JPEG chroma subsampling to retain critical edges (like your eyes, nose, and signature strokes) while aggressively compressing non-essential areas like solid background colors.
        </p>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">Which Photo Types Work Best at 50KB?</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          Not all images are meant to be 50KB. Understanding what works at this highly restricted file size can save you a lot of frustration:
        </p>
        <ul class="seo-list" style="gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 32px;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
            <svg style="flex-shrink: 0; color: var(--color-lime); margin-top: 4px;" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
            <div>
              <strong>Passport-Sized Headshots:</strong> These work exceptionally well. Because the subject (your face) fills the frame against a mostly solid background (like white or light blue), the compression algorithm can highly optimize the background data and allocate the remaining bytes to keeping your facial features sharp.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
            <svg style="flex-shrink: 0; color: var(--color-lime); margin-top: 4px;" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg>
            <div>
              <strong>Scanned Signatures:</strong> Signatures are ideal for 50KB compression. They typically consist of black or blue ink on a white background. By converting the image to grayscale or increasing contrast before compression, a signature can look flawlessly sharp at 20KB to 50KB.
            </div>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
            <svg style="flex-shrink: 0; color: #ff4a4a; margin-top: 4px;" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line></svg>
            <div>
              <strong>Landscapes and Dense Documents:</strong> These do not fare well at 50KB. A wide landscape with trees, sky, and varied textures contains too much complex data. Similarly, scanning a full A4 page of dense, small text and forcing it into 50KB will likely render the text completely unreadable.
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">Optimal Starting Dimensions for Maximum Clarity</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          If you take a photo with a high-megapixel camera (say, 8000x6000 pixels) and demand it be compressed to 50KB without resizing the dimensions, the resulting image will be a distorted, blocky mess. For aggressive 50KB compression, <strong>pixel dimensions matter just as much as file size</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="seo-p">
          To get the best possible quality out of 50KB, you should aim to pre-crop your image or use starting dimensions that align with the final output. The sweet spot for a 50KB headshot is generally between <strong>140x180 pixels and 350x450 pixels</strong>. If you provide an image within this dimensional range, our compressor won't have to waste valuable kilobytes defining thousands of unnecessary pixels, resulting in a crisp, clean photo that easily meets portal guidelines.
        </p>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">50KB vs 100KB vs 200KB: When to Use Which Limit</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          Navigating the maze of upload limits can be confusing. Here is a definitive breakdown of when you should use our different target sizes:
        </p>
        <ul class="seo-list" style="gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 32px;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
            <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: bold; color: var(--color-lime); margin-top: 2px;">50KB</div>
            <p class="seo-p-small" style="margin:0;">
              <strong>Use For:</strong> Profile photos, admit card headshots, and signature uploads for major Indian exams (UPSC, SSC, IBPS, NEET). It is meant exclusively for small, highly focused subject matter.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
            <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: bold; color: var(--color-lime); margin-top: 2px;">100KB</div>
            <p class="seo-p-small" style="margin:0;">
              <strong>Use For:</strong> Uploading single-page scanned documents like an Aadhaar Card copy, PAN card scan, or a simple 10th/12th mark sheet. It provides enough data allowance to keep standard-sized text readable.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
            <div style="background: var(--color-surface-3); padding: 4px 8px; border-radius: 4px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: bold; color: var(--color-lime); margin-top: 2px;">200KB</div>
            <p class="seo-p-small" style="margin:0;">
              <strong>Use For:</strong> High-density, multi-page PDFs, detailed academic transcripts, or professional resumes. At 200KB, complex graphics, logos, and dense paragraphs remain perfectly sharp and professional.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="seo-h3">Pro-Tips for Getting a Clear Face Photo at Just 50KB</h3>
        <p class="seo-p">
          If your photo is currently failing to look clear after compression, follow these targeted tips before putting it through our 50KB optimizer:
        </p>
        <ul class="seo-list" style="gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 32px;">
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
             <div style="font-weight:bold; color:var(--color-lime);">1.</div>
             <div><strong>Crop Tight:</strong> The most crucial step! Crop out your shoulders, chest, and the excessive background above your head. Frame the image so your face occupies 70% to 80% of the visual space. This forces the compressor to preserve your facial data rather than the wallpaper behind you.</div>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
             <div style="font-weight:bold; color:var(--color-lime);">2.</div>
             <div><strong>Use a Plain Background:</strong> A textured background (like trees or a brick wall) requires significant data to render. Stand in front of a flat, evenly lit white or light blue wall. A solid color compresses incredibly efficiently, leaving more data available for your face.</div>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
             <div style="font-weight:bold; color:var(--color-lime);">3.</div>
             <div><strong>Ensure Bright, Even Lighting:</strong> Shadows across your face create gradients, which eat up file size. Face a window or use a ring light so your face is illuminated evenly, which reduces complex noise and grain that would otherwise bloat the KB size.</div>
          </li>
          <li style="display: flex; gap: 12px; align-items: flex-start;">
             <div style="font-weight:bold; color:var(--color-lime);">4.</div>
             <div><strong>Strip Pre-existing Noise:</strong> If your original photo is heavily filtered or has digital grain from a low-light camera, run it through a basic noise reduction tool first. Clean pixels compress significantly better than grainy pixels.</div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="seo-p">
          With these strategies and our highly calibrated algorithmic compression, you will never have to worry about a portal rejecting your submission again. Your application will look professional, your features will be crystal clear, and your file size will stay comfortably under the strict 50KB threshold.
        </p>
      `}
    />

    <!-- Technical Note -->
    <div style="max-width: 860px; margin: 64px auto 0; padding: 40px; background: var(--color-surface-1); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 16px;">
       <h3 style="font-size: 1.125rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;">
         <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-lime)" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/></svg>
         Optimal 50KB Compression
       </h3>
       <p style="color: var(--color-ink-2); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.7;">
         The 50KB limit is one of the most common requirements for image uploads. PdfAndImg uses a Smart Compression Algorithm to find the highest quality that fits into 50KB, ensuring your file is ready for any application.
       </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
